WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
EU PROSECUTION:
Have you noticed that almost all big court rulings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:09.160
against tech monopolies and for tech regulation&nbsp;
comes from the EU? Within just the last 6 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:14.360
European countries have taken action against&nbsp;
Google 7 times, Apple 2 times, Facebook 3 times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:20.200
Microsoft 1 time, and Amazon 1 time. This usually&nbsp;
consists of billions of dollars worth of fines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:25.320
lawsuits regarding anti competitive practices,&nbsp;
investigations into power abuse, and of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:31.840
new standards for Apple. And those are just the&nbsp;
most notable cases from the EU. For perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.640
the biggest lawsuit that American authorities have&nbsp;
brought against big tech in recent times is suing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.440
to block Microsoft from acquiring Activision&nbsp;
Blizzard. And, in the end, this didn’t really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.520
do anything anyway given that Microsoft ended up&nbsp;
closing on the deal later the same year. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:51.600
the EU has often been described by the press&nbsp;
as being the last regulatory body that’s truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.640
fighting to keep big tech in check. But while&nbsp;
their efforts have painted as somewhat of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.480
valiant hero, this relentless pursuit has not&nbsp;
been without consequences. For example, Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:08.120
has not big tech companies of their own. In fact,&nbsp;
the only 2 European tech companies that even make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:15.400
the Fortune 100 are ASML and SAP. In contrast, 7&nbsp;
of the top 10 largest companies in the world are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:20.600
American big tech companies. At this point, you&nbsp;
can even argue that Berkshire Hathaway itself is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:26.680
just a tech holding company given that almost half&nbsp;
of their portfolio is just Apple stock. Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:31.280
is also getting the short end of the stick when&nbsp;
it comes to big tech compensation with big tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:38.080
companies regularly paying Europeans 25-50% less&nbsp;
for the exact same jobs. Yet despite all of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.360
the EU has continued their noble pursuit&nbsp;
of maintaining free markets and minimizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:49.840
monopoliziation. Just last month for example, the&nbsp;
EU started investigating Elon Musk’s X. But why?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:55.320
Does the EU truly just care that much about their&nbsp;
people and corporate landscape? Or is there some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:09.428
more ulterior motives at play? Well, here’s the&nbsp;
real reason why the EU keeps prosecuting big tech.

00:02:09.428 --> 00:02:11.520
LEFT BEHIND:
Taking a more macro perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.400
it’s likely that Europe’s moves against big&nbsp;
tech are less of an offensive move and more of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:21.800
a defensive move. You see, Europe has been getting&nbsp;
left behind within the tech industry since really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:28.360
the 1960s and 1970s, the era in which Silicon&nbsp;
Valley was born. We saw the rise of gen 1 tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:34.920
companies like Fairchild Semiconductor, Intel,&nbsp;
AMD, IBM, Microsoft, and Apple. This was largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:40.600
thanks to the high risk high reward mindset of&nbsp;
Americans across the board. In terms of company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:44.480
leadership, you had a bunch of 20 year olds&nbsp;
with no experience dropping out of college and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.680
betting it all on a company. In terms of venture&nbsp;
funding, Silicon Valley almost created a sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.560
self sustaining funding model. The winners of&nbsp;
each decade would go onto become billionaires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:03:01.120
and establish their own VCs and incubators that&nbsp;
supported a whole new generation of startups. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:06.080
in terms of regulation, all of the laws were set&nbsp;
up to reward winners as much as possible while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:11.840
also subsidizing losers as much as possible. This&nbsp;
meant lower taxes for companies, less regulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:16.400
government grants, tax credits, political&nbsp;
favors, and so on. On the flip side, if your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.400
venture didn’t work out, you still enjoyed solid&nbsp;
bankruptcy protections, the ability to try again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:27.000
and sometimes even government bailouts. This sort&nbsp;
of landscape was simply pushed into overdrive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.240
when President Ronald Reagan came into power and&nbsp;
implemented his controversial Reaganomics. He cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:40.320
the personal income tax rate from 70% down to 28%,&nbsp;
the corporate tax rate from 45% down to 35%, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.560
he would be dubbed the Great Deregulator. Whether&nbsp;
these moves were actually better for America over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:51.240
the long term or not are highly debated, but&nbsp;
one thing is for sure, these moves basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:56.960
paved the road for trillion dollar giants and mega&nbsp;
billionaires. At the same time, Europe was taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:02.120
a far more conservative approach. They stuck&nbsp;
with their high taxes, focused on social welfare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:07.480
maintained tight regulations, and they were&nbsp;
largely risk averse. In fact, to this day, 8 out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:12.720
of the top 10 countries with the highest personal&nbsp;
income tax rates are European countries. 9 out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.560
the top 10 countries with the highest sales tax&nbsp;
rates are also European countries. And social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:24.960
security tax can be as high as 65-68% in Europe.&nbsp;
What this means is that it’s not uncommon for even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:30.280
middle class Europeans to pay over half of their&nbsp;
income to various taxes. And if you’re a wealthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:36.680
European, you could be paying as much 70-80% of&nbsp;
your income to various taxes. For contrast, 40%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:42.760
of US households don’t pay any federal income tax&nbsp;
whatsoever, and the highest income earners often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:49.160
get away with the lowest tax rates. Again, whether&nbsp;
this is healthy or not is highly contested,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:54.000
but one things that’s for sure is that it’s far&nbsp;
better to be a rich person in America than a rich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.920
person in Europe. And as such, taking stupid high&nbsp;
risks to become stupid rich is far more appealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:05.920
to Americans. In Europe, it simply makes far more&nbsp;
sense to reduce risk with increasing net worth as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:11.000
more and more of your income will be handed over&nbsp;
to the government anyway. For the longest time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:16.640
this difference in mindset didn’t really incite&nbsp;
an economic difference. The American tech industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:21.800
was just a niche portion of the economy while the&nbsp;
real heavy weights were pharmaceuticals, banks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:26.640
retail giants, industrials, and energy, sectors&nbsp;
in which Europe had a bunch of their own heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:33.040
weights. But, all of this changed with the turn of&nbsp;
the century after which tech became the dominant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:38.840
player. Since then, Europe has been falling behind&nbsp;
across the board whether it be return on invested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:45.280
capital, growth, capital investment, or R&amp;D. And&nbsp;
all of these shortfalls are almost single handedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:51.240
due to just tech. In fact, McKinsey estimates&nbsp;
that over 90% of the shortfall in return on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:56.400
invested capital, over 60% of the shortfall in&nbsp;
growth, over 80% of the shortfall in investment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:03.000
and over 70% of the shortfall in R&amp;D are due&nbsp;
to Europe’s tech inferiority. To make things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:15.663
worse, big tech and America have been&nbsp;
playing Europe for fools for decades.

00:06:15.663 --> 00:06:18.200
PLAYED FOR FOOLS:
While Europe has been quite successful at taxing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:24.400
their own citizens and companies, the same cannot&nbsp;
be said about American companies who have insanely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:29.880
sophisticated ways to avoid taxes. Take Apple for&nbsp;
example. They like to hold the vast majority of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:34.760
their intellectual property in countries with&nbsp;
extremely favorable tax laws like Ireland. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:38.800
way, they can minimize their international&nbsp;
tax load by funnelling the majority of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:44.840
profits through these tax havens. Last year for&nbsp;
example, Apple made just over $110 billion out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:50.440
which $70 billion went through Ireland. It’s&nbsp;
the same thing with Microsoft, Google, Meta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:55.280
and any other big tech company you can think&nbsp;
of. Schemes like these are why Amazon was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:00.600
to get €44 billion worth of sales from Europe&nbsp;
without paying a dime in corporate tax. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:05.600
to be fair, big tech companies leverage these&nbsp;
same strategies to avoid American taxes as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:11.000
usually at even larger scales, but it’s a&nbsp;
completely different dynamic. For starters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:15.760
these companies employ millions of Americans who&nbsp;
earn multi six figures who end up paying a crap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:21.400
ton in income tax, sales tax, property tax, social&nbsp;
security tax, and so on, even with America’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:27.760
more favorable tax laws. But let’s even put tax&nbsp;
revenue. Even if big tech companies paid identical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:34.080
taxes to America and Europe, the 2 are not even&nbsp;
comparable. It’s almost like comparing Jeff Bezos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:40.840
to the average American. Before Jeff retired,&nbsp;
he was only earning a modest $81,840 on paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:45.840
an amount that’s quite comparable to the average&nbsp;
American. Now of course, you’re probably saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:50.640
that Jeff was earning tens of billions through&nbsp;
stock appreciation and you’re completely right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:56.120
So, let’s say that Jeff wasn’t allowed to sell any&nbsp;
of his $100 billion Amazon forune similar to how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.120 --> 00:08:01.960
America isn’t able cash in on the profits earned&nbsp;
by big tech. Jeff would still not even be close to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:07.800
being comparable to the average American despite&nbsp;
only earning $80k. This is because Jeff would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:12.480
still have leadership over a trillion dollar&nbsp;
company, influence over a million employees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:18.080
control over a third of the global cloud market,&nbsp;
and sales of over $500 billion just to name few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:22.760
perks. Also, he could borrow against his&nbsp;
Amazon fortune to fund an insane lifestyle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:28.880
without selling a cent of Amazon stock. It’s the&nbsp;
same case with America as a whole, just at a much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:35.120
larger scale. Sure, they may not make much tax&nbsp;
revenue from big tech, but their still the home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:41.200
country to tech companies worth tens of trillions&nbsp;
with dominant control over the entire globe’s tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:48.160
and internet. The raw amount of power, influence,&nbsp;
wealth, dominance, and control that America get’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:54.040
from big tech on a macro economic and political&nbsp;
scale is incomprehensible. China saw that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:58.880
is where things were heading back in the 1990s,&nbsp;
and that’s why they banned most American tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:04.720
giants and created their own tech giants. Europe&nbsp;
on the other hand largely ignored this trend and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:10.400
did virtually nothing to cultivate their own tech&nbsp;
hub. 20 years later, this has left Europe in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:16.920
much less desirable of simply being a consumer of&nbsp;
tech as opposed to being an owner of tech. And if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:21.960
we look back at history, the key to power and&nbsp;
wealth has been control over capital throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:27.680
the entirety of human history. From the ancient&nbsp;
kings who owned entire regions of land to the 18th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:32.120
century land owners who forged fortunes using&nbsp;
slave labor to the gilded age industrialists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:39.040
who controlled every sector known to mankind, the&nbsp;
key was always capital. For the past 1000 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:45.160
Europe has been the leader of global capital, but&nbsp;
over the past few decades, they have effectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:50.640
dropped the ball in regards to tech capital. And&nbsp;
now, they’re trying to claw back as much tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:59.120
capital as possible with the help of regulatory&nbsp;
control bringing us to modern day Europe.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:05.840
EUROPE’S LAST STAND:
With all of this extra context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:11.360
it becomes apparant that all of the EU’s lawsuits,&nbsp;
regulations, and fines are less of a heroic effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:16.320
to keep big tech in check and save the world or&nbsp;
something and more of a last ditch effort to save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:21.360
themselves in the new global order. Bill Gates&nbsp;
has long speculated that Europe is on it’s way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:27.960
becoming an open air museum for Americans and East&nbsp;
Asians. And honestly, this reality is looking more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:33.640
likely by the day. They’ve been falling behind&nbsp;
for half century, they’ve transitioned from being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:38.760
owners of capital to consumers, and now, they’re&nbsp;
using their last political resources to simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:44.240
delay the inevitable because let’s be honest,&nbsp;
that’s all that’s happening. I mean, do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:49.280
really think Apple has any less global power just&nbsp;
because they had to switch to USB C earlier than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:54.040
they would’ve wanted to or that Google or Facebook&nbsp;
really care about the multi billion euro fines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.040 --> 00:11:00.080
that cost a few weeks worth of profit. Of course&nbsp;
not, this is just the cost of doing business and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:05.680
maintaing international relations. Also, about&nbsp;
taxes, Europe has been trying to get big tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:10.160
to pay more taxes through new regulation.&nbsp;
And these efforts have been successful at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:16.320
generating more tax revenue but there’s just&nbsp;
one problem. It’s not the big tech companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:22.760
that are paying these taxes, it’s Europeans as big&nbsp;
tech companies are happy to directly pass on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:29.760
cost to European consumers. That’s why iPhones&nbsp;
cost hundreds of dollars more in Europe. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:35.120
the sad reality is that Europe’s antics against&nbsp;
big tech is not only not hurting or even slowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:39.560
down these giants but oftentimes, their&nbsp;
policies are backfiring and ending up costing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:45.400
Europeans. None of this is to say that Europe&nbsp;
is gonna end up as the 3rd world or something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:52.400
but a fate like Japan seems increasingly likely.&nbsp;
A stagnant region that is cashing in on the glory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:58.240
days of the past. Do you see a different outcome&nbsp;
for Europe? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:02.640
if you’d like to learn more about Europe’s rapidly&nbsp;
crumbling tech salaries, check out this video.

